# Part Four — The Seven Topic Dossiers

> Read each dossier just before you begin its topic. It tells you what the syllabus asks, what the examiner has asked, what the record reveals, what is new, what is likely next, the evidence you must carry, and exactly what the topic's two documents will teach.

## T1 · Gender Studies and the Social Construction of Gender

### What the syllabus asks for

Two heads, ten lines. **Head I**: an introduction to Gender Studies; the difference between Gender and Women's Studies; the multidisciplinary nature of the field; the autonomy versus integration debate; the status of Gender Studies in Pakistan. **Head II**: historicizing constructionism; problematizing "sex" through queer theory; whether sex is socially determined too; masculinities and femininity; and the nature versus culture debate in gender development.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 1

### What the record reveals

- **This is the second-heaviest topic (16.5 visits) and the most repetitive.** The discipline half (Head I) has four families — Women's vs Gender Studies, the multidisciplinary nature, autonomy vs integration, and status in Pakistan — asked 14 times between them, often two families in one question (2016, 2018, 2019, 2023).
- **The construction half (Head II) is where the difficulty is rising.** It moved from "what are the theories of social construction?" (2016) to "deconstruct the word gender" (2020), to "masculinities — natural or social?" (2021), to queer theory applied to Pakistan's transgender law (2026).
- **The examiner loves combined questions.** Nearly half of T1's questions join two families. Prepare each family so that it can stand alone *and* be paired.
- **The discipline half has been silent since 2023** — three years without autonomy/integration or status in Pakistan after seven appearances. Expect it back.

### The contemporary layer

The Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2018 and the Federal Shariat Court's judgment of ==19 May 2023== (sections on self-perceived identity and inheritance held repugnant to Islam; an appeal before the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court was still pending in 2026); the global "gender ideology" debate; masculinity in crisis and online misogyny; gender in school textbooks; the funding and growth of Gender Studies departments under the Higher Education Commission.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

Blind spot: **historicizing constructionism** by name. Near-blind: "is sex socially determined?" as a full question (only a short note, 2024). Likely next:

1. "Trace the historical development of social constructionism in the study of gender. How has it changed our understanding of 'woman'?"
2. "Critically evaluate the achievements and challenges of Gender Studies as an academic discipline in Pakistan."
3. "Is biology destiny? Discuss the nature versus culture debate with reference to gender roles in Pakistani society."
4. "Explain Connell's concept of hegemonic masculinity. How does it help explain gender inequality in Pakistan?"
5. "Queer theory argues that even 'sex' is socially constructed. Critically examine this claim."

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Robert Stoller (1968); Ann Oakley (1972) | The sex/gender distinction — first in psychology, then in sociology
Simone de Beauvoir (1949) | "One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman"; woman as "the Other"
Margaret Mead (1935) | *Sex and Temperament in Three Primitive Societies*: the Arapesh, Mundugumor and Tchambuli show "masculine" and "feminine" traits vary by culture
Berger and Luckmann (1966) | *The Social Construction of Reality* — the general theory behind constructionism
Women's Studies (1969–70) | First course at Cornell (1969), first programme at San Diego State (1970); US National Women's Studies Association (1977)
Bowles and Klein (1983) | *Theories of Women's Studies* — the autonomy versus integration debate
Pakistan (1989) | Women's Studies centres set up by the federal government in five universities; today degree programmes at many universities
Foucault (1976); Butler (1990); Sedgwick (1990); de Lauretis (1991) | Power and sexuality; performativity; the homo/heterosexual binary; "queer theory"
Laqueur (1990); Fausto-Sterling (1993, 2000) | The one-sex model of history; the "five sexes" and the social decisions in classifying intersex bodies
West and Zimmerman (1987); Bem (1974) | "Doing gender"; androgyny and gender schema
Connell (1987, 1995) | Hegemonic, complicit, subordinated and marginalised masculinities
Supreme Court (2009); Transgender Act (2018); FSC (2023) | Recognition of the khawaja sira; self-perceived identity; the Islamic-law challenge
:::

### What the T1 documents will carry

::: table | T1KB — chapter plan (about 18,500 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 30 key terms; the sex/gender picture; the whole topic on one page
Ch. 1 | What Gender Studies is — definitions, scope, aims and significance; why the discipline was needed
Ch. 2 | From Women's Studies to Gender Studies — the history since the 1960s; the differences on eight criteria; how they relate; men's studies and LGBTQ studies
Ch. 3 | The multidisciplinary nature — what each discipline contributes (ten disciplines); multi- vs inter- vs trans-disciplinary
Ch. 4 | The autonomy versus integration debate — Bowles and Klein; the case for each; the "dual strategy"; its contribution to knowledge; lessons for Pakistan
Ch. 5 | Gender Studies in Pakistan — history, institutions, research, achievements, problems and the way forward
Ch. 6 | Historicizing constructionism — from essentialism to constructionism; Mead, Beauvoir, Berger and Luckmann, Oakley, Foucault; the theories of social construction (socialisation, social learning, cognitive development, gender schema, doing gender, performativity)
Ch. 7 | Deconstructing "gender" — its many meanings; gendered language in English and Urdu
Ch. 8 | Problematizing "sex" — queer theory; is sex socially determined too? Laqueur, Fausto-Sterling, Butler; intersex; the counter-arguments
Ch. 9 | Masculinities and femininities — Connell's typology; emphasised femininity; where traditional roles come from; Pakistani examples
Ch. 10 | Nature versus culture — the biological case, the cultural case, interactionism; what the debate means for policy
Ch. 11 | The transgender debate in Pakistan — 2009, 2018, 2023; the arguments; the balanced view
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
:::

T1QA will answer all ==16 full questions== and the 2024 short note on "is sex also a social construct?".

## T2 · Feminist Theories and Practice

### What the syllabus asks for

One head, seven lines: what feminism is, and six schools — liberal, radical, Marxist/socialist, psychoanalytic, men's and postmodern feminism.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 2

### What the record reveals

- **Light in the descriptive paper, heavy in the objective paper.** Only seven visits (the lightest topic), but ==26== of 140 MCQs — second only to T1 — and 12 of the 40 MCQs in 2025–2026.
- **The examiner prefers comparison and application to recitation.** "How is radical feminism different from socialist feminism?" (2016); "which feminism can elevate women's status in Pakistan?" (2018); "describe any two Western schools" (2022).
- **Intersectionality has arrived.** Not in the syllabus, yet asked as theory in 2025 and applied to Pakistan in 2026. It is the single most important addition to this topic.
- **Two schools have never been asked:** men's feminism (never) and liberal feminism on its own.

### The contemporary layer

Is feminism a "Western import"? — the answer from South Asian history (Bhasin and Khan) and from Muslim feminists (Wadud, Mernissi, Riffat Hassan); intersectionality in Pakistan; ecofeminism and climate justice; the online backlash against feminism and the "manosphere"; men as allies (HeForShe, 2014).

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "What is men's feminism? Can men be feminists? Discuss with reference to masculinity studies."
2. "Liberal feminism is the most influential but the most criticised school of feminist thought. Discuss."
3. "Compare the explanations of women's oppression offered by radical and Marxist feminism. Which is more relevant to Pakistan?"
4. "Postmodern feminism denies that there is a single category 'woman'. Is this a strength or a weakness for feminist politics?"
5. "Feminism is often dismissed as a Western concept. Critically examine this view in the South Asian context."

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Liberal | Wollstonecraft (1792); Mill (1869); Friedan (1963); the US National Organization for Women (1966); equal rights, education and law
Radical | Millett, *Sexual Politics* (1970); Firestone, *The Dialectic of Sex* (1970); "the personal is political" (Carol Hanisch, 1969–70); patriarchy as the root oppression
Marxist | Engels (1884): "the world historical defeat of the female sex"; women's oppression began with private property and the monogamous family
Socialist | Hartmann (1979): dual systems; Juliet Mitchell (1971, 1974); domestic labour debate; wages for housework campaign (1970s)
Psychoanalytic | Freud's Oedipus complex and "penis envy"; Horney's "womb envy"; Chodorow (1978); Mitchell (1974); Lacan, Irigaray, Kristeva, Cixous
Men's feminism | Connell; Michael Kimmel; bell hooks, *Feminism Is for Everybody* (2000); UN Women's HeForShe (2014); MenEngage
Postmodern | Butler (1990); Cixous, "The Laugh of the Medusa" (1975); Irigaray; Kristeva; Lyotard's distrust of grand narratives
Intersectionality | Crenshaw (1989); Collins's matrix of domination (1990); the Combahee River Collective (1977)
Postcolonial | Mohanty, "Under Western Eyes" (1984/86); Spivak (1988)
Muslim feminism | Amina Wadud, *Qur'an and Woman* (1992); Fatima Mernissi, *The Veil and the Male Elite* (1987; English 1991); Riffat Hassan
:::

### What the T2 documents will carry

::: table | T2KB — chapter plan (about 17,500 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 30 key terms; the family tree of feminism; the four questions every school answers (cause, site, remedy, method)
Ch. 1 | What is feminism — definitions, core beliefs, myths about the word, feminism in South Asia
Ch. 2 | Liberal feminism — origins, thinkers, claims, achievements, critique, relevance to Pakistan
Ch. 3 | Radical feminism — patriarchy, sexuality, the body; cultural vs radical-libertarian; critique
Ch. 4 | Marxist and socialist feminism — Engels; the domestic-labour debate; dual systems; critique; the 2021 question in full
Ch. 5 | Psychoanalytic feminism — Freud and his critics; object-relations theory; the French school; critique; the 2021 question in full
Ch. 6 | Men's feminism — pro-feminist men, masculinity studies, HeForShe; can men be feminists?
Ch. 7 | Postmodern and post-structural feminism — difference, discourse, deconstruction; strengths and dangers
Ch. 8 | Beyond the syllabus — intersectionality and Black feminism; postcolonial; ecofeminism; Muslim feminism; standpoint theory; the concepts the MCQs ask (kyriarchy, strategic essentialism, patriarchal bargain)
Ch. 9 | The schools compared — one master table; which feminism suits Pakistan (2018)
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
:::

## T3 · Feminist Movements — the West, the United Nations and Pakistan

### What the syllabus asks for

One head, three strands: the first, second and third waves of feminism in the West; the United Nations conferences on women; and feminist movements in Pakistan.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 3

### What the record reveals

- **Two families carry the topic:** the waves (five times, including one exact repeat) and the movement in Pakistan (four times, plus APWA/WAF and NGO-isation).
- **The examiner always wants the link.** Four of the five waves questions ask how the waves influenced Pakistan, or how they affected women's lives. Western history alone is half an answer.
- **The Pakistani movement is asked with a twist each time:** strengths and weaknesses (2016), past versus emerging trends (2024), the shift from "legal rights" to "bodily autonomy" (2026), and NGO-isation (2026).
- **The UN conferences have never been a descriptive question**, although the MCQs love them.

### The contemporary layer

Aurat March (since ==8 March 2018==) and the controversy over its slogans; the fourth wave and the reception of #MeToo in Pakistan; Beijing+30 (2025); CEDAW reporting and the committee's observations on Pakistan; NGO-isation and donor dependence; digital activism and online backlash; the global anti-gender movement.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "Critically evaluate the contribution of the four UN World Conferences on Women to the global women's movement. What has Beijing+30 revealed?"
2. "Is there a fourth wave of feminism? Discuss its features and its expression in Pakistan."
3. "The Aurat March has divided Pakistani society. Discuss its demands, its critics and its significance."
4. "Discuss the role of the Women's Action Forum in the struggle against discriminatory laws in the 1980s."
5. "How far has the women's movement in Pakistan succeeded in translating activism into law?"

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
First wave | Seneca Falls (1848) and the Declaration of Sentiments; NUWSS (1897) and WSPU (1903); New Zealand (1893); UK (1918, 1928); USA (1920)
Second wave | Martha Lear names the "second wave" (1968); Friedan (1963); NOW (1966); Equal Pay Acts (US 1963; UK 1970); *Roe v. Wade* (1973)
Third and fourth waves | Rebecca Walker, "Becoming the Third Wave" (1992); Riot Grrrl; #MeToo (Tarana Burke 2006; global 2017)
The UN | CSW (1946); International Women's Year (1975); Mexico City (1975), Copenhagen (1980), Nairobi (1985), Beijing (1995; 189 states; 12 critical areas); CEDAW (1979/1981); UNSCR 1325 (2000); UN Women (2010); SDG 5 (2015)
Pakistan: roots | Fatima Jinnah; Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz and Begum Shaista Ikramullah; women in the Pakistan Movement
Pakistan: phases | APWA (1949); MFLO (1961); 1973 Constitution; Hudood (1979) and WAF (1981); 12 February 1983; Beijing and NGOs (1990s); NCSW (2000); Women's Protection Act (2006); laws of 2010–2016; Aurat March (2018–)
Scholarship | Mumtaz and Shaheed (1987); Bhasin and Khan; Alvarez and Jad on NGO-isation
:::

### What the T3 documents will carry

::: table | T3KB — chapter plan (about 17,000 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 25 key terms; the wave metaphor and its critics; the whole topic on one timeline
Ch. 1 | The first wave — suffrage and legal personhood in the USA, Britain and New Zealand; suffragists and suffragettes
Ch. 2 | The second wave — work, family, sexuality and the body; its achievements and blind spots
Ch. 3 | The third wave — diversity, identity and culture; critique
Ch. 4 | The fourth wave — digital activism, #MeToo; features and critics
Ch. 5 | The waves compared — principles, goals, strategies and effects on women's lives (2025)
Ch. 6 | The United Nations and women — CSW, the four conferences, CEDAW, the reviews, UN Women, SDG 5, Women, Peace and Security
Ch. 7 | Roots of the movement in South Asia — reformers, women in the Pakistan Movement
Ch. 8 | The movement in Pakistan, phase by phase — 1947 to today; APWA and WAF compared
Ch. 9 | The influence of the Western waves on Pakistan; past versus emerging trends (2019, 2023, 2024)
Ch. 10 | NGO-isation — the debate, the WAD critique, the evidence (2026)
Ch. 11 | Evaluation — strengths, weaknesses, achievements and the way forward
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
:::

T3QA will answer all ==9 full questions== (the exact repeat of 2019 and 2023 answered once, with a note) and the APWA/WAF short note.

## T4 · Gender and Development

### What the syllabus asks for

One head, four lines, but the densest in the syllabus: colonial and capitalist perspectives of gender; gender analysis of four development theories (modernisation, world-system, dependency, structural functionalism); the WID, WAD and GAD approaches and the gender critique of structural adjustment; and globalisation and gender.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 4

### What the record reveals

- **The heaviest topic (17.5 visits), and the only one present in every paper.**
- **Three strong families:** WID/WAD/GAD (five times), colonial and capitalist perspectives (four times, 2020–2024 in a row), and globalisation (four times). Development theories come up three more times.
- **The examiner wants critique, not description:** "critically review", "critically analyse", "give a critical gender analysis", "critically evaluate". A description of WID, WAD and GAD without their limitations cannot score well.
- **The newest questions join theory to the present:** development as the "new name of coloniality" (2024), gendered globalisation and world-system theory (2025), climate change and ecofeminism (2026).

### The contemporary layer

The 2022 floods (a third of Pakistan under water; an estimated 650,000 pregnant women affected, according to UNFPA) and recurrent heat waves; the care economy and unpaid work; gender budgeting in Pakistan's budgets; the SDGs at mid-point; IMF programmes and austerity; platform and gig work; artificial intelligence and women's jobs.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "Explain world-system theory. How does it explain the position of women in the global economy?"
2. "Distinguish between practical gender needs and strategic gender interests. Why does the distinction matter for development planning in Pakistan?"
3. "What is gender mainstreaming? Critically evaluate its achievements since Beijing 1995."
4. "Assess the gendered impact of IMF-supported stabilisation programmes on women in Pakistan."
5. "Unpaid care work is the hidden foundation of the economy. Discuss with reference to feminist economics."

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Colonial | British reforms: the sati ban (1829), the Widow Remarriage Act (1856), the Child Marriage Restraint Act (1929); the "civilising mission"; Mohanty and Spivak on the colonial gaze; Lugones (2007) on the coloniality of gender; Escobar (1995) on development discourse
Capitalist | Engels (1884); Maria Mies (1986) on "housewifisation"; the domestic-labour debate; public/private split
Modernisation | Rostow, *The Stages of Economic Growth* (1960); "trickle-down"; blamed "tradition" for women's subordination
Dependency / world-system | Frank and Prebisch (core–periphery); Wallerstein (1974): core, semi-periphery, periphery; the gendered global assembly line
Structural functionalism | Parsons and Bales (1955): men's instrumental and women's expressive roles; critique as a justification of inequality
WID / WAD / GAD | Boserup (1970); the Percy Amendment (US, 1973); WAD's Marxist critique (late 1970s); GAD and DAWN (1984–85); Kate Young; Moser (1993); Molyneux (1985); Kabeer (1994, 1999)
SAPs | IMF/World Bank from the 1980s; UNICEF's *Adjustment with a Human Face* (1987); Diane Elson on male bias (1991)
Globalisation | Feminisation of labour (Standing, 1989); global care chains (Hochschild, 2000); export processing zones; remittances
Climate | Ecofeminism (d'Eaubonne, 1974; Shiva, 1988); feminist environmentalism (Agarwal, 1992); the 2022 floods
:::

### What the T4 documents will carry

::: table | T4KB — chapter plan (about 19,000 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 35 key terms; "development for whom?"; the whole topic on one diagram
Ch. 1 | What development is, and why gender matters to it
Ch. 2 | Colonial perspectives of gender — British India; the colonial gaze; reform and its limits (2020, 2023, 2024)
Ch. 3 | Capitalist perspectives of gender — Engels to Mies; development as coloniality (2022, 2023, 2024)
Ch. 4 | A gender analysis of modernisation theory (2019, 2021)
Ch. 5 | Dependency theory and gender
Ch. 6 | World-system theory and gender (2025)
Ch. 7 | Structural functionalism and gender (2019)
Ch. 8 | The development theories compared — one critical table (2024)
Ch. 9 | Women in Development (WID)
Ch. 10 | Women and Development (WAD)
Ch. 11 | Gender and Development (GAD); Moser's needs and roles; Kabeer's empowerment; gender mainstreaming — the three approaches compared
Ch. 12 | The gender critique of Structural Adjustment Policies (2022)
Ch. 13 | Globalisation and gender — benefits, harms, "gendered globalisation" (2017, 2018, 2019, 2025)
Ch. 14 | Gender, environment and climate change — ecofeminism, feminist environmentalism, Pakistan's floods (2026)
Ch. 15 | From the MDGs to the SDGs — the global goals and Pakistan
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
