## T5 · Status of Women in Pakistan

### What the syllabus asks for

One head, four lines: women's health, education, employment, and law.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 5

### What the record reveals

- **Health is the favourite line** (2017, 2023, 2024). The 2024 question even quoted a named doctor (Mahmoud Fathalla and "the road to maternal death").
- **Education has never been a full question** — the clearest blind spot in the paper.
- **The topic is where the newest questions land:** Pakistan's gender-gap ranking (2018, 2026), intersectional disadvantage (2026), the digital gender gap (2026).
- **This topic feeds every other.** Almost every applied question in T3, T4, T6 and T7 needs its data and laws.

### The contemporary layer

The Global Gender Gap Report 2026 (Pakistan ==143rd of 145==, up from last in 2025); female labour-force participation of ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25); female literacy about ==53%== (Census 2023); out-of-school girls; child-marriage laws in Islamabad (2025) and Punjab (2026); the digital gender gap (the GSMA's figures: the mobile-internet gap narrowing from ==38%== to ==25%==); maternal and mental health; the 2022 floods; inheritance in practice.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "Discuss the status of women's education in Pakistan. What are the causes of the gender gap, and how can it be closed?"
2. "Pakistan has a strong body of pro-women laws but weak implementation. Critically examine."
3. "Why is female labour-force participation in Pakistan among the lowest in the world? Suggest a policy response."
4. "Evaluate the rights of women under the Constitution of Pakistan and the Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961."
5. "Women's health in Pakistan is a development issue, not only a medical one. Discuss."

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Constitution (1973) | Art. 25 (equality; special provision for women allowed), 26–27 (no discrimination in public places and services), 32 (women in local government), 34 (full participation of women), 35 (protection of family, mother and child), 37(e) (maternity benefits), 38; reserved seats (Art. 51, 59, 106)
Health | Maternal mortality ratio ==186== per 100,000 live births (PMMS 2019); high anaemia and stunting; Fathalla's "road to maternal death" (1987)
Education | Literacy: about ==68%== men, ==53%== women (Census 2023); more than 25 million children out of school, most of them girls
Employment | Female LFPR ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25; Punjab highest, Balochistan lowest); women concentrated in agriculture and informal home-based work; 10% quota for women in federal services
Family law | Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act (1939); MFLO (1961); Family Courts Act (1964); child-marriage laws (Sindh 2013; Islamabad 2025; Punjab 2026)
Protective laws | Women's Protection Act (2006); harassment (2010); acid (2011); anti-women practices (2011); honour (2016); rape (2016, 2021); Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act (2020); domestic violence (provinces from 2013; Islamabad 2025)
Indices | Global Gender Gap 2026: 143rd of 145, 59.5%; economic participation 144th; education 130th; political empowerment 107th
Institutions | NCSW (2000); provincial commissions; Ministry of Human Rights; women's protection centres; BISP payments to women
:::

### What the T5 documents will carry

::: table | T5KB — chapter plan (about 17,000 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 30 key terms; how "status" is measured; the constitutional framework
Ch. 1 | Measuring women's status — the Global Gender Gap, GII and GDI; Pakistan's record since 2006 (2018, 2026)
Ch. 2 | Women's health — maternal, reproductive, nutritional and mental health; causes; policy; the way forward (2017, 2023, 2024)
Ch. 3 | Women's education — literacy, enrolment, dropout, higher education; causes; policy; the way forward
Ch. 4 | Women and employment — participation, sectors, the informal economy, wage gaps, harassment, care work; the double burden (2019)
Ch. 5 | Women and law — the Constitution; family law; criminal law from the Hudood Ordinances to the 2006 Act; protective laws; inheritance; the enforcement gap; human rights (2020)
Ch. 6 | Intersectional disadvantage — rural, minority, disabled and poor women (2026)
Ch. 7 | The digital gender gap — access, skills, safety; the 2024–25 progress (2026)
Ch. 8 | Climate change and women in Pakistan — floods, heat, health, livelihoods (2026, with T4)
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
