## T6 · Gender and Governance

### What the syllabus asks for

One head, six lines: defining governance; the suffragist movement; gender issues in women as voters, as candidates and as representatives; and the impact of the political quota in Pakistan.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 6

### What the record reveals

- **The quota is the heart of the topic** (2016, 2021, 2024, 2025), always with an evaluative twist: pros and cons, whether it shows results, its impact, its history.
- **Participation is asked as a value question** (2017: does it stabilise democracy?; 2018: should women have equal numbers?; 2020: should women "pull each other up"?; 2024: is participation a human right?).
- **Three syllabus lines are blind spots:** defining governance, women as voters, and women as candidates on their own.

### The contemporary layer

The 2024 general election (only ==11== women won general National Assembly seats); the Elections Act 2017 (section 206: at least ==5%== of general-seat tickets to women; section 9: the Election Commission may void a poll where women's turnout is below ==10%==); the gender gap in the electoral rolls (about 10 million more men than women registered in 2024); Maryam Nawaz as Pakistan's first woman chief minister (Punjab, 2024); the zero women ministers noted in the 2025 gap report; the 2026 bills for ==33%== women on federal boards.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "Define governance. Why is gender equality essential to good governance?"
2. "Discuss the gender issues faced by women as voters in Pakistan, with reference to electoral reforms."
3. "What obstacles do women face as candidates in Pakistan's elections? How can political parties help?"
4. "Descriptive representation does not guarantee substantive representation. Discuss with reference to women legislators in Pakistan."
5. "Trace the history of the suffragist movement. What lessons does it hold for women's political participation today?"

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Governance | UNDP (1997): participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus, equity, effectiveness, accountability, strategic vision; World Bank's six governance indicators
Suffrage | New Zealand (1893); Finland (1906: first to let women stand); UK (1918/1928); USA (1920); Millicent Fawcett (NUWSS) and Emmeline Pankhurst (WSPU)
Rights | UDHR Art. 21; ICCPR Art. 25; CEDAW Arts. 7–8; Beijing's critical area G ("women in power and decision-making")
Quota history | 1956 and 1962 constitutions (small reserved quotas); 1973 (10 seats); 1985 (20 seats); the provision lapsed and the 1990, 1993 and 1997 assemblies had no reserved seats; restored 2002 (60 NA, 17 Senate, 128 provincial — 132 after the FATA merger; 33% in local councils from 2000–2001); Elections Act 2017
Voters | Gender gap in electoral rolls (about 10 million, 2024); CNIC registration drives; 10% turnout rule
Representatives | Farzana Bari's assessment (2002); the Women's Parliamentary Caucus (2008); women's private members' bills
Leaders | Fatima Jinnah (1965 election); Benazir Bhutto (PM 1988–90, 1993–96); Fahmida Mirza (first woman Speaker, 2008); Maryam Nawaz (first woman CM, 2024)
Theory | Critical mass (about 30%; Dahlerup); descriptive vs substantive representation (Pitkin, 1967); Hoodfar and Tajali (2011) on quotas
:::

### What the T6 documents will carry

::: table | T6KB — chapter plan (about 16,000 words).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Part | Content
Zero | The ABC: 25 key terms; the ladder of participation from voter to leader
Ch. 1 | Defining governance — and why it is gendered
Ch. 2 | The suffragist movement — Britain, the USA, New Zealand; South Asia; lessons (2019)
Ch. 3 | Political participation as a human right (2024)
Ch. 4 | Gender issues in women as voters
Ch. 5 | Gender issues in women as candidates
Ch. 6 | Gender issues in women as representatives (2022)
Ch. 7 | Quotas — theory and types; the history in Pakistan; impact; pros and cons (2016, 2021, 2024, 2025)
Ch. 8 | Women in leadership — does it help to "pull each other up"? (2020); women and democracy and development (2017, 2018)
Parts Two–Five | Contemporary debates · record and five likely questions · one-liners · revision sheet
