## T7 · Gender-Based Violence and the Three Case Studies

### What the syllabus asks for

Two heads. **Head VIII**: defining gender-based violence; theories of violence against women; structural and direct forms of violence; strategies to eliminate violence against women. **Head IX**: case studies of Mukhtaran Mai, Malala Yousafzai and Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy.

### What the examiner has asked

@py topic_questions 7

### What the record reveals

- **The most regular family in the paper.** Violence against women appears in nine of eleven papers; "forms and strategies" alone five times.
- **The questions escalate in depth:** forms (2016, 2017), structural and direct with a case study (2019), power and control (2020), the Punjab Act of 2016 (2024), root causes and how they intersect (2025), masculinity studies (2026).
- **Each case study has been asked once**, always as "discuss the background" or "critically examine", never as a bare biography.
- **Law is now named in the question** (the Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016, in 2024). Knowing the laws section by section pays.

### The contemporary layer

The Islamabad Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act 2025; online harassment and blackmail under PECA 2016; honour killings and femicide in HRCP data; jirga "justice"; masculinity and violence; Mukhtaran Mai's schools, Malala's global advocacy and the Malala Fund, Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's later work.

### Blind spots and five likely questions

1. "Explain Galtung's concepts of direct, structural and cultural violence with examples from Pakistan."
2. "Critically evaluate Pakistan's legal framework against gender-based violence. Why does violence persist?"
3. "Discuss the Mukhtaran Mai case as an example of the failure and the resilience of justice in Pakistan."
4. "How did Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's documentaries influence legislation in Pakistan?"
5. "Technology-facilitated violence is the new frontier of GBV. Discuss with reference to Pakistan."

### The core evidence in one page

::: table
@widths 4.3,9.7
Evidence | What to carry into the exam
Definitions | UN Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women (1993); CEDAW General Recommendations 19 (1992) and 35 (2017)
Scale | WHO: about 1 in 3 women worldwide experience physical or sexual violence in their lifetime; HRCP annual honour-killing figures
Theories | Feminist/power and control (Duluth wheel, 1981); social learning (Bandura); the cycle of violence (Walker, 1979); resource theory (Goode, 1971); the ecological model (Heise, 1998)
Forms | Galtung: direct (1969), structural (1969), cultural (1990); the life-cycle of violence from sex-selection to elder abuse
Laws | Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2004 (honour; vani); Women's Protection Act 2006; harassment (2010); acid (2011); anti-women practices (2011); honour (2016); rape (2016, 2021); PECA (2016); Punjab PPWA (2016); domestic violence (Sindh 2013, Balochistan 2014, KP 2021, Islamabad 2025)
Mukhtaran Mai | 22 June 2002, Meerwala (Muzaffargarh); a panchayat-ordered gang rape; six convicted by the anti-terrorism court (2002); five acquitted by the Lahore High Court (2005); acquittals upheld by the Supreme Court (2011); her schools and welfare organisation
Malala Yousafzai | Born 12 July 1997, Mingora (Swat); BBC Urdu diary (2009); shot by the TTP on 9 October 2012; UN speech on 12 July 2013; Malala Fund (2013); Nobel Peace Prize (2014) at 17 — the youngest laureate
Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy | *Saving Face* (Oscar, 2012) on acid-attack survivors — Pakistan's first Oscar; *A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness* (Oscar, 2016) on honour killing, which spurred the October 2016 anti-honour-killing law
:::

